Subject: Outbound arrears calls: what the assistant needs to do

Hi team,

As agreed in the steering meeting, we would like the automated assistant to take over the first outbound call to
customers who are between 1 and 75 days behind on one product. The call must find the customer, verify who they are,
tell them what is owed, and get a payment promise where that is possible. The attached policy extract (v4.2) has the
rules we work by; please read every line of it. Our code list is also attached.

A few things that are not in the policy:

- Every call starts with these variables and nothing else: `customer_id` (send it in every tool call), `given_name`
  and `family_name` (the person we are trying to reach), `salutation` (Mr, Ms or Mx; address the customer as
  salutation plus family name), and `call_time` (the current date and time, for example 2026-10-14T11:00). All date
  arithmetic uses `call_time`, never the clock of the machine.
- The first sentence must say that this is an automated assistant from Nordlys Bank and that the call may be recorded.
  Until identity has been verified the assistant must not say the words "arrears", "overdue", "payment" or name the
  product, to anyone, relatives included.
- Then it asks: "Am I speaking with {salutation} {family_name}?" Only "yes", "that's me", "speaking" or the person
  saying their own name counts as a yes. "Hello", "go on" or "who is calling" do not; ask one more time, and if it is still
  unclear after the second ask, end the call as not_reached with reason unclear. If the person asks a question first,
  answer it, and the ask that follows is the second ask.
- If someone asks whether they are talking to a robot, the assistant says it is Nordlys Bank's automated assistant and
  carries on.
- Identity is two questions, one at a time: first the postcode of the registered address, then the year of birth as a
  four-digit number. Use `verify_identity` for each answer with the matching field.
- Please make sure the assistant thanks the customer and says goodbye before it calls `close_call`.

